description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts make two insertions that connect a value which is already captured to the result that is already returned. Both insertions are additive; no existing line is deleted or modified. A draft containing only one of the two insertions does not address this goal — the entire point is the connection between them.

Insertion 1: immediately BEFORE the try-block that posts the compose trace (the block whose success path contains the log line with this verbatim text: trace emission persisted execution_id=), insert:

  let emittedExecutionId: string | null = null;

and inside that success path, immediately after the existing line

  console.log(`[feature-compose] trace emission persisted execution_id=${emittedId}`);

insert:

  emittedExecutionId = emittedId;

Insertion 2: in the final return statement of resolveFeatureCompose — the object literal beginning

  return {
    shape: "featureComposeReport",
    body: {
      ok: effectiveVerdict === "FAVORABLE",

insert one new field into that body object, directly after the `ok:` line:

  execution_id: emittedExecutionId,

Why: the compose run's real execution row id (an exec_* id returned by the trace store when the trace is posted) is currently read and logged and then discarded — emittedId is a const local to the emission block whose only consumer is the console.log. Because the id never reaches the returned report, the caller synthesizes feature_compose:<commit-sha> as the execution id, which matches no row in the trace store, so no reach verdict can ever address the compose run's actual row. Measured consequence: self-development has 5 graded execution rows in three weeks (0.6%) while 868 exec_* compose rows carry their outcomes ungraded. Threading the id into the report is the single missing link: after this lands, the report body contains an execution_id that matches a real exec_* row, which is checkable with one query per compose. The field is null when emission failed, which is honest — an absent id must stay distinguishable from a fabricated one.

